<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
	Who may see and change what in E-Home (haumacher/ehome #48, #152): the roles, the groups and rules
	that give them, and the grants on the ehome.* modules. Administrators (root) bypass all of it.

	Access per project (haumacher/ehome #152). A project names the accounts holding a role in it
	(ehome.project:Project#members, #editors, #owners, de.haumacher.ehome.project.ProjectMembers), and a
	user sees a project exactly when holding one of the three roles there; there is no global role that
	reads or writes a project:
	- ehome.Owner: everything, including the members (the operation ManageMembers) and deleting the
	  project. Whoever creates or imports a project becomes its owner (de.haumacher.ehome.project
	  .ProjectOwners); a project keeps at least one owner.
	- ehome.Editor: reads and edits the project with everything in it (buildings, group addresses,
	  devices, functions, controllers), imports into it, programs its devices on the bus, registers its
	  controllers, sets their tokens and deploys runtime images to them.
	- ehome.Viewer: reads the project with its controllers, changes nothing and neither programs devices
	  nor deploys.
	Role rules give these roles along the three references: every member holds ehome.Viewer on the
	project, the editors ehome.Editor, the owners ehome.Owner. The members themselves are written only
	by the member commands of the owner (in Java, which keep the invariants), nobody through the model.

	Global roles, held on the security root:
	- ehome.Installer, through the group "ehome.installers": may create and import projects (Create on
	  ehome.project:Project, checked on the security root) and edits the shared master data. Nothing
	  more: no project is read or written through it. An administrator adds an account to the group.
	  The group "ehome.viewers" of before has no meaning any more and is no longer created: the
	  migration Issue_152_Project_members made its members viewers of the projects that existed then;
	  an administrator may delete it.
	- SecurityStructure.user, the engine's role of the group "user" every new account joins: every
	  account reads the shared master data (manufacturers with their catalogs and application programs,
	  datapoint types, mask versions, the Zigbee catalog, the device templates), the security root and
	  the values of the enumerations, and uses the assistant.
	The device templates (haumacher/ehome #103) are written by administrators only: their grants name no
	role for Write, Create and Delete.

	Members of a project see each other (haumacher/ehome #152, point 5a): an account holds
	ehome.Collaborator on the accounts that share a project with it, by a role rule on tl.accounts:Person
	whose path goes from the account back to the projects naming it as a member and on to their members.
	The role reads the name of such an account (the user name, the first and the last name) and its mail
	address, which the contact of the account holds (a second rule on Contacts:PersonContact along the
	same path, one step longer), and nothing else of them; Discover follows from these attribute grants.
	The attribute grants replace the class grant of their attributes, so they name the engine's
	PersonalProfile as well, with which an account reads its own. The role is not transitive and ends
	with the last shared project. The owner adding a member chooses among these accounts (the options
	are the accounts the owner discovers) or names an account by its mail address or user name.

	The roles reach the objects in two steps (access-configuration article):
	- The installers hold their role on the security root (singleton rule), and the five singletons of
	  the shared master data name the security root as their role parent; they decide by the grants
	  below. A project has no role parent: its roles are the ones its role rules give. An installer's
	  Create of a project is decided on the security root, where the engine checks the creation of an
	  object without container. A role parent would hand the installer's Create to every project, and
	  every right on an object implies Discover on it: the installer would find every project in the
	  lists. The coverage analysis does not count the security root for a Create and would report the
	  grant as dead; the rule ehomeInstallerOnOwnedProject works around it (doc/tl-todo.md).
	- Every other E-Home type is held in a composition of one of them and delegates the whole decision
	  to its container (the default access parent of a composition part): a device, a group address, a
	  function, a deployment or a controller is read where its project is read and changed where the
	  project is written; a catalog entry or an application program where the manufacturers are; a
	  Zigbee device type with its function entries where the Zigbee catalog is; a device template with
	  its object overlays where the device templates are.
	- Every controller is a part of exactly one project (ehome.project:Project#controllers, haumacher/ehome
	  #151), the one on the LAN as well as the enrolled one; whoever sees the project sees its
	  controllers, nobody else. A controller without a project, which only the migration of existing
	  data leaves (one no project deployed to), has no container to take its access from: nobody but an
	  administrator sees it, who assigns it to a project or deletes it (Administration, E-Home
	  controllers).

	The bearer token of a controller (ehome.runtime:Controller#token) is never readable through the
	model: its read grant lists no role, which denies the read for every user but an administrator.
	An editor sets it (the write follows the controller); the deployment reads it in Java
	(de.haumacher.ehome.runtime.Deployer), which the model access check does not cover. The views show
	only whether a token is set. The secret of an enrolled controller (ehome.runtime:Controller#secret,
	haumacher/ehome #54) is handled the same way: E-Home generates it at the enrollment and checks the
	controller's authentication with it in Java (de.haumacher.ehome.connection); nobody enters or reads
	it. The private installation keys of a project (ehome.project:Project#installationKey and
	#newInstallationKey, homecontrol issue #35) are handled the same way: E-Home generates them and signs
	the runtime images with them in Java (de.haumacher.ehome.connection.InstallationKeys); the views show
	only the fingerprints of the public keys. The open enrollment code of a project (ehome.project:Project#enrollmentCode) is read by
	its owners and editors only: whoever knows it can enroll a controller for the project.

	The access tokens of the agent access (module tl-layout-view-agent, tl.agent:AccessToken,
	doc/agent-access.md) are their owner's: an account holds ehome.TokenOwner on the tokens it issued
	(a role rule along AccessToken#owner) and reads and withdraws them in the dialog "Agent access" of
	the development menu; nobody else sees them but an administrator, who lists all tokens in the
	administration. The token is issued and checked in Java (IssueAccessTokenAction, AgentAccess), which
	the model access check does not cover; only the hash of its secret is stored. The two transient
	types of the dialog (the form of a new token and the invitation shown once) are never persisted and
	are marked internal.
-->
<application config:interface="com.top_logic.basic.config.ApplicationConfig$Config"
	xmlns:config="http://www.top-logic.com/ns/config/6.0"
>
	<services>
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialRolesManager">
			<instance>
				<roles>
					<role name="ehome.Installer"/>
					<role name="ehome.Owner"/>
					<role name="ehome.Editor"/>
					<role name="ehome.Viewer"/>
					<role name="ehome.Collaborator"/>
					<role name="ehome.TokenOwner"/>
				</roles>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialGroupManager">
			<instance>
				<groups>
					<group name="ehome.installers"/>
				</groups>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.manager.AccessManager">
			<instance>
				<role-rules>
					<rules>
						<singleton-rule id="ehomeInstallersOnRoot"
							role="ehome.Installer"
							target="SecurityStructure#ROOT"
						>
							<group-with-name name="ehome.installers"/>
						</singleton-rule>
						<!--
							Workaround of a false finding of the coverage analysis (doc/tl-todo.md, "Create of a
							top-level type"): the Create of a project is decided on the security root, which the
							analysis does not count, and reports the grant to ehome.Installer as dead unless a rule
							delivers the role on the type. The owners of a project hold it there: it grants nothing
							on an existing project they do not hold already (Create only, and Discover, which every
							member has).
						-->
						<rule id="ehomeInstallerOnOwnedProject"
							meta-element="ehome.project:Project"
							role="ehome.Installer"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#owners"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<!-- The roles in a project, along the references naming its members (haumacher/ehome #152). -->
						<rule id="ehomeProjectOwner"
							meta-element="ehome.project:Project"
							role="ehome.Owner"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#owners"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="ehomeProjectEditor"
							meta-element="ehome.project:Project"
							role="ehome.Editor"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#editors"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="ehomeProjectViewer"
							meta-element="ehome.project:Project"
							role="ehome.Viewer"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#members"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<!--
							Members of a project see each other (haumacher/ehome #152): from an account back to the
							projects naming it as a member, on to their members, who hold the role on the account.
						-->
						<rule id="ehomeCollaboratorOnAccount"
							meta-element="tl.accounts:Person"
							role="ehome.Collaborator"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#members"
									inverse="true"
								/>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#members"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<!-- The same for the contact of the account, which holds its mail address. -->
						<rule id="ehomeCollaboratorOnContact"
							meta-element="Contacts:PersonContact"
							role="ehome.Collaborator"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.accounts:Person#contact"
									inverse="true"
								/>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#members"
									inverse="true"
								/>
								<step attribute="ehome.project:Project#members"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="ehomeOwnerOfAccessToken"
							meta-element="tl.agent:AccessToken"
							role="ehome.TokenOwner"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.agent:AccessToken#owner"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
					</rules>
				</role-rules>
				<security-parents>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForManufacturers"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.catalog:ManufacturerRoot"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForDatapointTypes"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.datapoint:DatapointTypes"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForMaskVersions"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.operational:MaskVersions"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForZigbeeCatalog"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.zigbee.catalog:ZigbeeCatalog"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForDeviceTemplates"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.template:DeviceTemplates"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<!-- The values of the enumerations, see the grant of tl.model:TLClassifier below (TL #30275). -->
					<rule id="ehomeRootForClassifier"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="tl.model:TLClassifier"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
				</security-parents>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.model.security.SecurityConfigurationService">
			<instance>
				<security-config>
					<!--
						The modules of five of the six types deciding for themselves; the sixth, the root
						of the mask versions, has grants of its own below. The types of the other modules
						(ehome.building, ehome.logical, ehome.physical, ehome.operational, ehome.monitor,
						ehome.runtime, ehome.zigbee) are composition parts and delegate to their containers.
					-->
					<!--
						A project: read by its members, written by its editors and owners, deleted and its
						members managed by its owners (haumacher/ehome #152). Created by an installer, on the
						security root.
					-->
					<class name="ehome.project:Project">
						<operations>
							<operation name="ManageMembers"/>
						</operations>
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.Owner, ehome.Editor, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant operation="Write" roles="ehome.Owner, ehome.Editor"/>
						<grant operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Owner"/>
						<grant operation="ManageMembers" roles="ehome.Owner"/>
					</class>
					<!-- The members are written by the member commands only (Java, keeping the invariants). -->
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#members">
						<grant operation="Write"/>
					</part>
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#editors">
						<grant operation="Write"/>
					</part>
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#owners">
						<grant operation="Write"/>
					</part>
					<module name="ehome.catalog">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="SecurityStructure.user, ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<module name="ehome.datapoint">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="SecurityStructure.user, ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<!-- The root of the shared mask versions, the one type of ehome.operational no composition holds. -->
					<class name="ehome.operational:MaskVersions">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="SecurityStructure.user, ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</class>
					<module name="ehome.zigbee.catalog">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="SecurityStructure.user, ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<!--
						The device templates (haumacher/ehome #103): the singleton decides, its templates and their
						overlays are composition parts. Read by every account, written by administrators only
						(haumacher/ehome #152): no role is granted Write, Create or Delete.
					-->
					<module name="ehome.template">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="SecurityStructure.user, ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<!--
						The security root stands for E-Home as a whole: every account reads it (the shell offers
						the assistant to who reads it). A command that reads no object of the model checks the
						project it acts for or the right to create projects instead (haumacher/ehome #65, #152).
						The root has no grants of its type; the operation Administration declared for it by the
						engine stays with the administrators.
					-->
					<singleton name="SecurityStructure#ROOT">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="SecurityStructure.user, ehome.Installer"/>
					</singleton>
					<!-- A read grant without roles: nobody but an administrator reads the token. -->
					<part name="ehome.runtime:Controller#token">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<!-- The same for the secret of an enrolled controller (haumacher/ehome #54). -->
					<part name="ehome.runtime:Controller#secret">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<!--
						The private installation keys of a project (homecontrol issue #35): nobody but an
						administrator reads them; E-Home signs with them in Java.
					-->
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#installationKey">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#newInstallationKey">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<!-- An agent access token: its owner reads and withdraws it. -->
					<class name="tl.agent:AccessToken">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.TokenOwner"/>
						<grant operation="Delete" roles="ehome.TokenOwner"/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.agent:NewAccessToken" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.agent:IssuedAccessToken" internal="true"/>
					<!--
						The AI modules (haumacher/ehome #126, #129). Their conversations, memories and personal
						access tokens are their owner's by the policies the modules ship
						(tl-ai-core.chatSecurity.config.xml, tl-ai-core.accessTokens.config.xml). The types
						below are read and written by the modules' code and by administrators only: the form of
						a new personal access token (transient, never persisted), the agents and skills authored
						in the running application (E-Home's agents are configured, administrators author
						further ones), the pages an agent publishes (the group "workspace", granted to nobody),
						and the period of the agent monitor of the administration (transient).
					-->
					<class name="tl.ai.access:AccessTokenRequest" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.core:Agent" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.core:Skill" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.htmlpages:GeneratedHtmlPage" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.monitor:Period" internal="true"/>
					<!--
						The values of the enumerations (a schedule's weekdays, the kind of a function, ...) are
						model data both roles read, or the MCP tools leave them out of every object a user reads
						(haumacher/ehome #141). Workaround of TL #30275: the engine decides the read of a
						classifier by roles instead of treating it as model data (doc/tl-todo.md).
					-->
					<class name="tl.model:TLClassifier">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="SecurityStructure.user, ehome.Installer"/>
					</class>
					<!-- The open enrollment code of a project: its owners and editors only, a viewer must not enroll a controller. -->
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#enrollmentCode">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.Owner, ehome.Editor"/>
					</part>
					<!--
						Members of a project see each other (haumacher/ehome #152): the name and the mail address of
						the accounts sharing a project, nothing else. These grants replace the class grant of their
						attributes, which PersonalProfile holds for the own account (personalProfileSecurity.config.xml,
						personalProfileContactSecurity.config.xml of the engine): it is named again.
					-->
					<part name="tl.accounts:Person#name">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="PersonalProfile, ehome.Collaborator"/>
					</part>
					<part name="tl.accounts:Person#firstName">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="PersonalProfile, ehome.Collaborator"/>
					</part>
					<part name="tl.accounts:Person#lastName">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="PersonalProfile, ehome.Collaborator"/>
					</part>
					<part name="tl.accounts:Person#contact">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="PersonalProfile, ehome.Collaborator"/>
					</part>
					<part name="Contacts:PersonContact#email">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="PersonalProfile, ehome.Collaborator"/>
					</part>
				</security-config>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The assistant (haumacher/ehome #126, doc/ai.md): which agents, models and tools a run of the
			chat may use, by the roles held on the security root (ai/security/tool-access-configuration,
			ai/security/run-access). What a tool may then read or change is decided per object by the
			grants above - since the roles per project (haumacher/ehome #152), by the role of the user in
			each project: a viewer's tools read the projects the viewer sees and change none of them, an
			editor's write tools change the projects the editor edits - always after the user approved
			the call.

			- Every account (SecurityStructure.user, the engine's group "user" every new account joins) and
			  the installers: reading the model and the data (model.read, data.read) and changing data with
			  the object tools (data.write: updateObject, createObject, deleteObject, editText), within the
			  grants above.
			- Not granted to anybody: changing data with a TL-Script (script, changeData). E-Home's
			  TL-Script functions are not access checked and include writing to the bus (monitorSend),
			  which no approval of a data change should cover; E-Home's own tools (haumacher/ehome #127,
			  #128) check the project and join data.read and data.write. Equally egress (the web),
			  workspace, and the privileged groups (model.structure, agent.authoring).
			- An account in none of the groups: nothing beyond the implicit grants (asking the user, loading
			  a skill), and no agent at all.

			The roles run the chat's agent (TL_SingleAgent) and no other; the model is the operator's
			choice through tl_anthropic_models and tl_ai_default_model, so every model a provider offers
			may be picked.

			A run without anybody to approve its calls - a call of an external agent over the MCP endpoint
			(ehome.mcp.config.xml, haumacher/ehome #129) - is capped by the background allowlist. It admits
			changing data: an editor's MCP client (Claude Code) changes data as the editor could in the
			chat, and the client asks its user before a call the tool list marks destructive. The grants
			still decide: a viewer's client is refused every change of the project. E-Home starts no agent
			on a schedule.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.security.AiToolAccessService">
			<instance>
				<tool-access>
					<role name="SecurityStructure.user">
						<grants>
							<grant group="model.read"/>
							<grant group="data.read"/>
							<grant group="data.write"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
					<role name="ehome.Installer">
						<grants>
							<grant group="model.read"/>
							<grant group="data.read"/>
							<grant group="data.write"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
				</tool-access>
				<baseline/>
				<background-allowlist>
					<grant group="model.read"/>
					<grant group="data.read"/>
					<grant group="data.write"/>
				</background-allowlist>
				<agent-access default="none">
					<role name="SecurityStructure.user">
						<grants>
							<grant agents="TL_SingleAgent"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
					<role name="ehome.Installer">
						<grants>
							<grant agents="TL_SingleAgent"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
				</agent-access>
				<model-access default="all"/>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!-- The operation of a project's owner managing its members (haumacher/ehome #152). -->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.simple.CommandGroupRegistry">
			<instance>
				<group name="ManageMembers"
					type="write"
				/>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The check covers the E-Home modules only: the modules of the engine have no complete access
			definition of their own (8.0.0-alpha11), and E-Home neither shows nor grants their types to
			its users (see doc/tl-todo.md, "Access coverage of engine modules").
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.element.boundsec.manager.coverage.SecurityCoverageCheck">
			<instance excluded-modules="Contacts,SearchExpressionStructure,SecurityStructure,Tag,mandatorStructure,orgStructure,tl.accounts,tl.comments,tl.contact.ui,tl.core,tl.folder,tl.model,tl.reporting,tl.search,tl.units"/>
		</config>
	</services>
</application>
